module bindings.javascript.multipart_field_name_nesting_depth_limit;

// A `multipart/form-data` part carries a client-chosen field *name*, and every
// multipart parser in this ecosystem hands that name to the application
// verbatim: busboy, formidable, multiparty and dicer all document a `field`
// event whose first argument is the raw name off the wire. Middleware that
// wants HTML's nested-name convention -- `a[b][c]` -- then feeds that name to a
// path expander, whose documented job is to create one container per path
// segment: `append-field`'s `appendField(store, key, value)` ("sets a value on
// an object, adding array parts as it goes"), `dset`, `set-value`,
// `object-path`'s `set`, `dot-prop`'s `set`, lodash `_.set`/`_.setWith`. The
// number of objects allocated and the number of walk steps taken are therefore
// both the number of bracket groups the client typed, and a client can type a
// few thousand of them inside a field name of a few kilobytes -- so a small
// request buys deep-nested allocation, once per part, for as many parts as the
// part limit allows (CWE-400/CWE-770).
//
// A cap on the field name's *length* is not a cap on its depth: a bracket group
// costs three characters, so any name-size limit generous enough to be usable
// still admits hundreds of levels. The remediation is a separate ceiling on the
// number of levels, which is what this ecosystem calls a depth or nesting limit
// -- `qs` and `body-parser` document `depth`, glob and GraphQL matchers
// document `maxDepth`/`depthLimit`. The negation therefore names that quantity
// in the spellings a parser would use, whether it is read off an options object
// or held in a local counter, rather than any one library's identifier.
//
// The module is the unit. The expansion happens inside the parser's `field`
// callback, but that callback cannot see the event registration or the
// content-type test that make the name untrusted -- both sit in the enclosing
// function -- while a ceiling may equally be enforced in the callback or before
// the parser is constructed. The two literal clauses are what keep this a
// statement about a multipart body parser rather than about every module that
// calls a path setter: `set`-family helpers are everywhere, and outside a
// multipart field handler their path is usually a constant the program wrote
// itself.
//
// Every clause is a node-derived token and none of them reads the module's own
// prose. At module scope `containsAny(node.context.literal, ...)` reaches the
// raw source and a comment can satisfy it, so the two literal clauses are
// written as exact `in [...]` tests, which cannot be; the negations use
// `selector` and `identifier`, which are comment-proof at module scope.
binding jsMultipartFieldNameNestedExpansionWithoutDepthLimit {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "module"
    and node.context.language == "javascript"
    and node.context.literal in ["field"]
    and node.context.literal in ["multipart"]
    and containsAny(node.context.callPath, ["appendField", "dset", "setValue", "deepSet", "objectPath.set", "dotProp.set", "_.set", "lodash.set", "_.setWith"])
    and not (containsAny(node.context.selector, ["Depth", "depth", "Nesting", "nesting", "maxLevels", "MaxLevels", "levelLimit", "LevelLimit", "maxSegments", "MaxSegments", "segmentLimit", "SegmentLimit", "maxBrackets", "MaxBrackets", "bracketLimit", "BracketLimit", "maxKeys", "MaxKeys"]))
    and not (containsAny(node.context.identifier, ["Depth", "depth", "Nesting", "nesting", "maxLevels", "MaxLevels", "levelLimit", "LevelLimit", "maxSegments", "MaxSegments", "segmentLimit", "SegmentLimit", "maxBrackets", "MaxBrackets", "bracketLimit", "BracketLimit", "maxKeys", "MaxKeys"]))
  emit issue code.UnboundedAllocationReview at node
}
